<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slavko Kvaternik</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Slavko Kvaternik was a Croatian army officer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> leader who proclaimed the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> on 10 April 1941, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> forces invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. He served as its deputy leader and commander of the armed forces until 1942. He was tried and executed in Yugoslavia in 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slavko-kvaternik.jpg" alt="Slavko Kvaternik" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Croatian military photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marshal_Slavko_Kvaternik.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Slavko Kvaternik was born on 25 August 1878 in Moravice, then called Komorske Moravice, in the Kingdom of Croatia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. His father Ljudevit was a postman, and his mother Marija, née Frank, was Catholic and of German descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. He was educated at a military academy and served in the Austro-Hungarian Army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, receiving the German Iron Cross 1st Class in 1918. He married Olga Frank, daughter of the Croatian nationalist politician Josip Frank, who was of Jewish descent and had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. Their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a> became a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustasha</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1918 Kvaternik joined the newly formed National Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and became its Chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a>. At the end of that year he commanded Croatian troops in the successful occupation of Međimurje. He later transferred to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-yugoslav-army">Royal Yugoslav Army</a>, where he stayed until 1921. In 1929 he was one of the founders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustasha</a>, the Croatian Revolutionary Movement, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> on 6 April 1941, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> formed their government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ante-pavelic">Ante Pavelić</a>. Four days later Kvaternik proclaimed the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and formed its first Ustasha government. At his request, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladko-macek">Vladko Maček</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-peasant-party">Croatian Peasant Party</a>, who had refused to head the new state for the Germans, told the people to cooperate with the new regime. Kvaternik was commander-in-chief of the armed forces, with the title of vojskovođa, or marshal, and was the second most powerful man in the state as a member of the Doglavnik's Council.</p><p>In May 1942 he ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> from 12 villages in Petrinja County and six in Glina County to the Jasenovac and Stara Gradiška concentration camps. About 1,200 to 1,250 people were deported from Petrinja County, and at least 471 of those deported from Glina County died in the camps.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Olga Frank had committed suicide at the end of August 1941, probably because of her Jewish origins and the roles of her husband and especially her son in the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. After the war Kvaternik was captured by the United States Army and extradited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, where he was tried and sentenced to death for crimes committed under the regime. He was hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> on 7 June 1947. He had received the Military Order of the Iron Trefoil, first class, which gave him the title of vitez, or knight.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Slavko Kvaternik” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 10 April 1941, with the German army in control of Zagreb, the retired lieutenant-colonel <b>Slavko Kvaternik</b> proclaimed the Independent State of Croatia in the name of Pavelić.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Moravice, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.43,15.01&amp;year=1922" aria-label="Moravice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Slavko Kvaternik"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4110 6132h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4110 6132h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.43,15.01&amp;year=1922">What was here in 1922? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
